            <ArticleTitle>Circulating Normal IgG as Stimulator of Regulatory T Cells: Lessons from Intravenous Immunoglobulin.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>789-792</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1471-4906(17)30172-2</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.it.2017.08.008</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG), a pooled normal IgG formulation prepared from thousands of healthy donors' plasma, is extensively used for the immunotherapy of autoimmune and inflammatory disorders. Recent reports demonstrate that IVIG exerts anti-inflammatory actions by stimulating the activation and expansion of regulatory T (Treg) cells by multiple mechanisms via antigen-presenting cells (APCs).</AbstractText>
